2610.03717v1,Less Decoder is More Encoder: Geometric Representation Learning from Novel View Synthesis,"Abstract


This paper examines the role of Novel View Synthesis (NVS) in geometric representation learning.","6 
Conclusion




While our current pretraining relies on posed video sequences and static, textured scenes, and our findings are bound by a fixed compute and data budget that has yet to reveal the full scaling laws of the architecture, SNAP demonstrates that feed-forward novel view synthesis (NVS) is a legitimate powerhouse for self-supervised geometric pretraining. Extending this paradigm to dynamic video remains a critical next step in establishing this approach as a foundation model. More broadly, our results show that NVS’s rich geometric signal is most available when two architectural pathologies are corrected: decoders expressive enough to absorb spatial reasoning that should belong to the encoder, and pixel-space targets that contaminate gradients with low-level statistics.






Restricting decoder receptive field via a 
BlockNN
 mask biases spatial reasoning back into the scene tokens; replacing pixel targets with last-layer DINOv3 features further redirects optimization toward a structured, geometry-privileged manifold. The resulting backbone rivals geometry-supervised models across five tasks without dense 3D annotations, and transfers robustly to robot manipulation, where viewpoint shifts break standard 2D semantic models. Moreover Appendix 
D
 suggests that these improvements are not restricted to a single teacher, improving correspondence among DINOv3, SAM, and MAE alike. Most strikingly, 
SNAP
 demonstrates NVS’s geometric signal is best leveraged by restricting decoder capacity, not by optimizing reconstruction, underscoring that representation quality does not always correspond with reconstruction fidelity.",cues
