Discussion




We have shown that undercomplete linear autoencoders trained with SGD display directed scale drift towards decoder-heavy weights. The endpoint of this drift is selected not by an equilibrium statistical mechanism like balancing of gradient noise 
(
Ziyin et al., 2025a
)
, but instead dynamically by the fact that scale drift increases curvature up to the point where the solution would become unstable.






From a broader optimization perspective, this resembles the general phenomenon of 
progressive sharpening
 in full-batch gradient descent, which posits that the top eigenvalues of the Hessian increase sequentially over training up to the stability threshold, after which they remain near the edge of stability 
(
Cohen et al., 2021
; 
Cohen et al., 2025
; 
Damian et al., 2023
)
. How to precisely define the edge of stability in the stochastic setting remains an active area of research 
(
Andreyev and Beneventano, 2025
; 
Emmanouilidis et al., 2026
)
. What is notable in our setting is the fact that the edge of stability is reached purely through a slow effective drift along a manifold of functionally-equivalent solutions, after the PCA solution manifold is reached. Therefore, progressive sharpening need not reflect continued learning of the function. Whether and how this phenomenology transfers to other optimization settings is an interesting direction for future work.






