Discussion




Overall, we propose an instrument that separates the three things a cross-framing translation can get wrong:
truth, coded on the candidate alone; content, as bidirectional entailment on the shared domain; and
scope, as the relation between the two domains of quantification, each in its own blind queue and
never averaged. A judge-free measure of the standing hypothesis, frozen before counting and
calibrated against a planted-positive control, accompanies it.






Across the seven models the recurring operation is object-level substitution: nouns and notation are
transported between framings even when the standing axioms are not. Whether the hypothesis is stated
is inherited from the surface of the task rather than derived from what the translation requires,
yielding a map on objects without the constraint that would make it a map on arrows; which surface,
this corpus cannot decide (§
4.5
). The error typology agrees: vertical failures register as generalised conclusions and coerced types
rather than as omitted hypotheses (Table 
5
). Truth, by contrast, is comparatively protected: scope survives in half of the rewrites whose source
would become false without the hypothesis and in at most an eighth of those where the widened
statement stays true of other objects (Table 
4
), so what escapes is a widening that provers, type-checkers and
equivalence-scored benchmarks all accept.
These findings are subject to three limitations. All seven models are general-purpose, and
mathematics-specialised models trained against verifiable reward

(
Shao et al., 2024
)
 or dedicated autoformalizers

(
Xuejun et al., 2025
)
 may behave differently. The effective clusters are six framing pairs, three in the benchmark and three from ProofWiki, the
last comprising 
7
7
, 
15
15
 and 
3
3
 items. Finally,
promotion rates are lower bounds with a measured recall, the absorption term is pattern-sensitive,
and the 
load
 annotation is annotator-dependent.






In conclusion, evaluations of mathematical reasoning score an answer against a fixed target, and
instruments that certify fidelity require a formal one; neither observes a statement whose level of generality must be recomputed when it moves between subfield dialects. Coding content and scope separately, we find that models preserve truth and content while failing
to carry generality: across seven models the
domain is widened in 
60.6
%
60.6\%
 of abstracting rewrites and in 
0.3
%
0.3\%
 of specialising ones; under a bare prompt the most capable model writes out the hypothesis that would have prevented it in only 8 of the 37 rewrites that require it (21.6%), and on human-authored statements it is carried across in 4.2%; and an explicit instruction raises that rate to 89.2% without making it track the requirement.
It replicates on items excluded by the pre-registered rule and on statements written by
mathematicians. We therefore argue that these systems have acquired an object-level dictionary
between framings rather than the constraint that carries axioms to theorems, a distinction no prover, type-checker or equivalence-scored benchmark reports. Its persistence in mathematics-specialised models, under instruction across models and on register-varied pairs is left to future work.






