Limitations.

Compiled GNNs produced by 
𝖦𝗋𝖺𝖼𝗋
\mathsf{Gracr}
 rely on binary embeddings and noiseless aggregations, and therefore have limitations in terms of realism compared to GNNs trained from data.
This is, however, a deliberate design choice allowing us to reason 
exactly
 about the internal workings of both the model and the explainer, at a level of detail previously not possible.
Nonetheless, experiments conducted on compiled models should be interpreted carefully, and evaluations based on them should be intended as a minimum bar rather than a proxy of in-production evaluations.
Furthermore, just like for trained models, our results are to be interpreted in conjunction with the model, as different model configurations can yield different results. Nevertheless, 
𝖦𝗋𝖺𝖼𝗋𝖡𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗁
\mathsf{Gracr}\mathsf{Bench}
 allows us to rigorously control these configurations – which are otherwise inextricably confounded on trained models – and assess the robustness of explainers across these.
Last, our current implementation is restricted to binary classification and categorical node features.






Future work.

