Conclusion




In this paper, we propose a W16A16 quantization scheme to execute deep neural networks, in particular time convolutional neural networks on MCUs. We provided the exact assembly instructions needed to run W16A16 quantization efficiently, implemented the quantization scheme as an open source package, and performed experiments to compare W16A16 quantization to W8A8 and W8A16 quantization across two hardware platforms and three machine learning tasks. Our results challenge the notion that lower quantization precision automatically leads to faster speed. Our W16A16 quantization method is both faster (ca. 17.6% for a full TCN model on STM32H7) and more energy-efficient than W8A16 across MCUs, at both layer-wise and end-to-end model level, while providing higher accuracy in both regression and classification tasks. While W8A8 is slightly faster and more energy efficient compared to our proposed W16A16 scheme, the empiric classification and regression performance of the quantized neural net is significantly worse (ca. 100x quantization error in regression tasks). Therefore, W16A16 provides an attractive option for applications with high accuracy requirements under tight energy and time constraints.






This work also provides the explanation for the empiric observations based on the underlying architecture: in the Cortex-M instruction set, the maximum throughput for integer MAC operations is the same for 8-bit and 16-bit operations and 8-bit operations require additional pre- and post-processing instructions, thus yielding overhead for W8A16 quantization.
Based on our architecture analysis, we modified the 64-bit requantization provided by CMSIS-NN, and designed a Full-Precision 96-bit Cross-Register Shift and Two-Stage saturation to avoid the overflow of intermediate values.






Further work could explore whether such architecture-specific resolution gains with little energy/time overhead can also be translated to other MCUs or hardware architectures.






5.1 
Limitations




The cost advantage of our proposed W16A16 quantization method is based on the architecture of ARMv7E-M which exhibits the same speed for 8-bit and 16-bit operations. However, this special effect does not necessarily translate to newer architectures such as ARMv8.1-M or RISC-V, which are capable to process four 8-bit data elements in a single instruction of MAC operation. Still, ARMv7E-M is a widely applied cortex-M architecture, providing a wide scope of application of our approach at this time.






We also note that our experiments are limited to TCN models and three machine learning tasks across two data sets. On other data sets, other tasks, and other models, results may be slightly different. Still, given the consistent patterns we observed across metrics, data sets, and hardware in our experiments, we are fairly confident that the qualitative pattern would generalize: W16A16 is faster than W8A16 and roughly comparable to W8A8, while W8A8 is significantly less precise compared to the other two.",cues
