In this paper, we empirically investigate the distinct roles that individual tokens play during video reasoning and propose a multi-role token selection mechanism coupled with a self-diagnostic rollout-contrastive intervention paradigm. Specifically, we introduce a proposal-policy collaborative learning framework operating at the token level. We first quantify the inherent limitations of high-entropy tokens in video understanding and reveal the functional complementarity between visual-aware and answer-aware activations through a systematic token distribution analysis. We further demonstrate that our self-diagnostic curriculum masking strategy can better and more reliably stimulate the model’s ability to capture fine-grained visual concepts and temporal dynamics, yielding robust gains under challenging conditions. Extensive experiments across multiple backbones, including ablations on scoring metrics, masking strategies, and curriculum directionality, as well as thorough comparisons with on-policy distillation methods, consistently demonstrate the effectiveness and superiority of our approach, establishing a new state of the art on key video reasoning benchmarks.",cues
