Discussion




We studied the role of clustering in causal effect identification from a theoretical viewpoint. We defined coherent clustering operations, which prevent clustering from creating directed cycles or introducing spurious directed paths. Whenever the clustering operation does not create a directed cycle, any causal effect that is identifiable in the clustered graph is identifiable in the original graph as well, and the identifying functional can be obtained from the clustered graph by a simple substitution. This reduces the problem of identification invariance: it suffices to determine when nonidentifiability in the clustered graph implies nonidentifiability in the original graph, or equivalently, when clustering cannot create a hedge that is not already present. We established three conditions based on c-components under which no such hedge is created, namely plainness, c-coherence and c-isolation, each of which renders the clustering of a coherent cluster identification invariant for all causal effects not involving the clustered variables.






These results widen the range of settings in which identification invariance can be used in practice. Coherent clusters generalize transit clusters: they impose no requirement that all members of the cluster that have observed parents outside the cluster must have the same observed parents outside the cluster. An analogous result applies to the children of the cluster. In 
Section
 
5
, we illustrated the practical applicability of the results.






Each of the three conditions, plainness, c-coherence and c-isolation, is sufficient but not necessary for universal identification invariance, and clustering operations exist that are identification invariant without satisfying any of them (e.g., 
Figure
 
4
). The conditions presented here were chosen to be structural properties of the cluster that can be readily verified from the original graph. Characterizing structural conditions that are both necessary and sufficient
remains an open question. The conditions may also be relaxed if invariance is sought only for a smaller family of causal effects (e.g., just one specific causal effect), and in such a setting even the coherence assumption may not be needed, as long as new directed paths are not formed between the variables of the causal effects under study.






We also required that clustering preserves acyclicity. Allowing clustering to create directed cycles could be a potential next step in future research. In this setting, cycles in the clustered graph does not need to correspond to any cycle in the original graph, and identification has been studied through the do-calculus applied directly to the clustered graph 
(
ferreira2025identifying
; 
yvernes2026relaxing
)
. Imposing conditions for identification invariance appears considerably harder in this case: the characterization of nonidentifiability itself has to be modified, as an ordinary hedge no longer detects the nonidentifiability that a cluster-level cycle induces 
(
ferreira2025identifying
)
.






Our focus has been on the theoretical properties of clustering operations. These properties provide a basis for future algorithmic solutions that could, for instance, detect a subset of coherent clusters or verify plainness, c-coherence and c-isolation of a cluster.






