Conclusion




We introduced 
HyperThink
, a text-to-parameter framework that amortizes long thinking traces into query-conditional parameter updates.
Rather than generating explicit thinking tokens at test time, 
HyperThink
 predicts a small, structured parameter shift for each query, enabling the base model to operate in the efficient 
non-thinking
 mode while emulating the reasoning ability of the 
thinking
 mode.
At the core of our approach is a bias-predicting hypernetwork equipped with a vector-quantized bottleneck, which constrains per-query adaptations to a finite set of reusable reasoning prototypes.
Empirically, 
HyperThink
 improves the low-latency region of the accuracy–latency trade-off on mathematical and general reasoning tasks, with its strongest gains in the near-non-thinking regime.










