Conclusion




In this work, we revisit a fundamental yet underexplored question in multimodal graph learning: whether a graph should be constructed at all. Our empirical study reveals a 
No-Free-Graph
 phenomenon, showing that graphification can bring substantial gains in some cases while providing limited or even negative benefits in others. Motivated by this observation, we propose 
MAG-Scout
, a pre-construction framework that estimates graphification value before the complete topology is materialized. 
MAG-Scout
 first extracts a compact relational sketch, then evaluates graphification from the perspectives of necessity, constructability, and utility, and finally combines the predicted value with construction cost and uncertainty to make a selective Build-or-Skip decision. Extensive experiments across diverse multimodal datasets, tasks, and graph constructors demonstrate that 
MAG-Scout
 can reliably identify when graphification is beneficial while avoiding unnecessary graph construction. We hope this study encourages multimodal graph learning to move beyond the assumption of always constructing graphs toward more selective and value-aware graphification.






