In this work, we developed a hybrid framework for reconstructing spatially varying complex-valued shear modulus functions from internal displacement measurements in a two-dimensional model of magnetic resonance elastography. The approach combines a convolutional neural network (CNN) with adjoint-based PDE-constrained optimization, using the CNN to provide a spatially informed initialization that is subsequently refined by a nonlinear conjugate-gradient method with Armijo line search. Numerical experiments demonstrate that the CNN provides useful initial reconstructions across different inclusion geometries and can generalize to configurations not encountered during training, while the PDE-constrained optimization improves reconstruction accuracy in the noiseless setting and enforces consistency with the governing physical model. Experiments with noisy measurements show that the CNN retains dominant spatial features under moderate perturbations, although the optimization becomes increasingly sensitive to noise and may introduce spatial artifacts, emphasizing the importance of appropriate regularization and stopping criteria. Overall, the results demonstrate the potential of combining data-driven initialization with physics-based optimization to reduce dependence on arbitrary initial guesses while retaining the interpretability of a mechanistic model. Future work will focus on noise-aware regularization, improved learning and optimization strategies, more heterogeneous coefficient geometries, and extensions to realistic three-dimensional MRE models.",cues
