Conclusion




We have developed a closure modeling framework for identifying missing components of dynamical systems using Kernel Ridge Regression (KRR).
We examined the proposed approach on two classes of closure problems.
The first arises in ODE and PDE settings, where the missing components take the form of difference equations.
The second arises from the moment closure problem in kinetic equations, where the closure model is formulated as a system of algebraic equations.






For the first class of problems, we derived an error bound for the proposed framework in an ODE setting and identified the contributions to the total error from the ODE solver, the approximation of the unresolved scales, and the interpolation of the unresolved-scale contributions needed to couple them to the resolved numerical solver.
We numerically tested the framework on two benchmark problems: the Lorenz-63 system and the Kuramoto–Sivashinsky equation.
In both examples, the proposed KRR framework produced accurate long-horizon predictions, with substantial improvements over the LSTM model.
Of the two kernels considered, the diffusion maps (DM) kernel yielded slightly more accurate predictions.






For the second class of problems, we considered moment closure for a one-dimensional kinetic equation.
In this setting, our closure approach models the discrepancy between the numerical transport flux constructed by a computationally expensive kinetic solver that preserves the discrete collision invariants using the conservative discrete Maxwellian of Mieussens 
[
29
]
, whose parameters are obtained using a nonlinear solver that matches the discrete conserved moments to the solver tolerance.
Our numerical experiments show that the predictive performance of the closure model is sensitive to the choice of model configuration.
Specifically, we considered three flux-correction models:
(1) a global model using KRR in PCA coordinates;
(2) a zeroth-order spatially local model using local resolved states as inputs;
and (3) a first-order spatially local residual model that augments the zeroth-order model with an additional learned correction using spatial differences of the resolved states.
Numerically, we found that the global model achieves high accuracy for unimodal initial conditions but is less accurate for bimodal initial conditions.
The zeroth-order local model improves the prediction accuracy, while the first-order local model achieves the highest accuracy on average across the two test families.
These results indicate that spatial localization is the principal source of the improved robustness observed in the kinetic experiments.
Practically, the proposed closure scheme allows the coupled system to be solved using only the corrected macroscopic solver, avoiding the expensive kinetic computation and the associated nonlinear discrete-equilibrium scheme 
[
29
]
 required to maintain discrete conservation in the kinetic solver.






Future work will investigate extensions of the framework to noisy data using the weak formulation recently proposed in 
[
19
]
.
An open question arising from this study, particularly from the empirical results for the Kuramoto–Sivashinsky equation, is why non-Markovian models do not appear to be essential within the proposed closure framework.
The numerical results in the kinetic problem motivate extensions to more challenging kinetic regimes, including nonsmooth flows, higher-dimensional problems, and more general collision models.








