In this paper, we presented a generic, model-agnostic coordinate regression module for 3D seismic horizon tracking that overcomes the key limitations of standard dense segmentation frameworks. By directly predicting continuous horizon depth coordinates along vertical seismic traces, our approach eliminates the need for artificial label-thickening, spatial upsampling, and heuristic post-processing extraction. Comprehensive evaluations across four distinct backbone architectures ranging from classical convolutional networks (ResNet-50, ResNet-101) to high-capacity modern vision models (ViT-Large, ConvNeXt-Large) demonstrate that replacing segmentation heads with our proposed regression module consistently yields superior quantitative performance and qualitatively smoother horizon picks.






Our findings further highlight three key operational advantages of the proposed formulation: (1) Incorporating an LSTM module across adjacent seismic slices effectively models spatial correlations, enforcing structural coherence along complex geological interfaces. (2) By training exclusively on valid picks and leveraging a geology-based consistency prior, the regression framework avoids the performance degradation caused by false-negative background assumptions inherent to segmentation masks in sparse-pick settings. (3) The model’s predictive uncertainty directly reflects underlying geological complexity, providing a reliable confidence metric to automatically highlight ambiguous structural zones for expert geoscientific review.






Ultimately, this work provides a flexible, plug-and-play alternative to dense segmentation for data-driven horizon tracking applications for automated seismic interpretation workflows.








