Within the reported scope, LLAV reconstruction is feasible but harder on complex colour datasets (RQ1), one-bit quartile binning gives the best fidelity-cost balance (RQ2), and teacher architecture affects task accuracy and reconstructability independently (RQ3). Clause pooling further shows reconstructed bits forming spatial evidence maps despite the ITMs having no explicit spatial prior.








VI 
Future Work




Several directions follow from this work.
First, reconstruction could extend from the final hidden interface to intermediate layers, toward a layer-by-layer clause-level account of the whole network, potentially with reconstruction-aware teacher training so hidden representations become recoverable by construction.
Second, clause banks could be coupled with satisfiability-based verification to state symbolic guarantees for safety-critical deployment.
Finally, the approach could scale to larger color benchmarks with channel-specific RGB evidence and imbalance-aware baselines, and generalize to tabular, sensor, biomedical, and time-series data, where a logic-native clause bank offers an accurate, low-complexity, and explainable alternative.








