By systematically investigating mathematical understanding in LLMs, we hope this work encourages a broader shift in AI for Science from optimizing benchmark performance alone toward identifying the capabilities required for scientific problem solving and discovery. Given the foundational role of mathematics across STEM disciplines, this structural perspective may extend beyond mathematics. We highlight three promising directions. First, our diagnostic framework could be generalized to other structurally rigorous domains, such as theoretical physics and computational chemistry. Second, the observed discovery-execution asymmetry suggests a potential design principle for mathematical agents: explicitly separating structural search from procedural execution may enable more effective allocation of inference-time computation. Finally, future work could explore integrating mathematical primitives with formal verification systems such as Lean, connecting informal structural reasoning with formal proof generation.",cues
