The remainder of this section establishes Theorem 13. The theorem converts five conditions
into a Wasserstein guarantee with an explicit shot count: the protected roots in the annulus, radial
separation of all roots from the two contour circles (the parameterq), a floor on the normalized
signal (the parameterb), operational access, and polynomially bounded parameters. The supporting
statements address the three places where noise can break the pipeline. First, phase unwrapping:
the logarithmic increments ofP are recovered from endpoint ratios only if these stay away from
the branch cut (Lemma 14 and Propositions 15 and 16). Second, shot noise: relative error in the
endpoint signals must neither change the branch assignment nor spoil the quadrature (Lemmas 17
to 19). Third, inversion: noisy Fourier data from the root moments must be turned into a measure
that is close in Wasserstein distance (Lemma 22 and Proposition 23). The proof of Theorem 13 at
the end of the section assembles these pieces. Proofs of the supporting statements are collected in
Section A.
