The last ingredient is the inversion step, which turns estimates of the root moments into a
measure that is close in Wasserstein distance to the thermal measure on the window. We use the
linear-programming estimator of Chen et al. [37], which has two parts. The Jackson kernel smooths
a measure at a controlled Wasserstein cost, so that a finite number of Fourier coefficients suffice
to determine it up to that cost. The linear program then searches for a measure whose smoothed
Fourier coefficients match the noisy data.
