6 Sample Complexity for Quadrature and Phase Unwrapping Error
The recovery theorem takes the signal floorb and the radial separationq as hypotheses. For a free
Hamiltonian both are automatic at a contour that is separated from the spectrum,β∆(µ)≥log (3M):
then every root satisfies the radial separation withq = e−β∆(µ)≤ 1/(3M), and the normalized signal
obeys|Ξ|≥ e−1 (Corollary 27). This section makes the resulting resource count explicit for one
contour, with all constants. As in the previous section, proofs of the supporting statements are in
Section A.
Three conditions on the number of quadrature pointsS enter below. The quadrature aliasing
error of Proposition 28 requiresS≥ 2k; the phase margin of Corollary 29 requiresS≥ 4M + 2; and
a root-moment error of at mostkRkτ on one circle, obtained from Proposition 30 with endpoint
precision τ/4, requiresS−k≥log (1 + 4M/τ )/log (3M). Hereτ is the relative accuracy of the root
moment on one circle; it is the quantity denotedτ in Theorem 13, whereasς denotes the relative
error of a single endpoint estimate. For convenience we define
