Proof. The reflections aboutΠ0 andΠ 1, and hence controlledGU, have polynomial-size circuits by
their definitions. By Corollary 47, the relevant phase boundaries have gap at leastmin{π/12,g/ 2}.
Thus Lemma 57, followed by a phase or a controlledX and uncomputation, implements2Π+−I,
FR, andFS to clean-workspace norm errorη with cost polynomial in|U|,m,g −1,log (1/η). These
operations preserve each eigenspace ofGU, so the error bound also holds for superpositions and
states entangled with an external reference.
ForUrot =f(K), useK4 = I. Two-bit phase estimation identifies each of1, i,− 1,− iexactly with a
constant number of controlled calls toK; apply the phasef and reverse phase estimation [36, Sec. 5.2].
Assign f(−1) = 1, since this eigenspace is absent. Replace each call toK = (2Π+−I )(2Π0−I )by
the compiled reflections above, using fresh clean work registers. A hybrid argument bounds the total
error by a constant multiple ofη.
Choose η = 2−r/C for a sufficiently large absolute constantC and composeFRFSUrot. Synthesis
over a fixed universal gate set adds only polynomial overhead inr [41]. Reversing or externally
controlling the same construction gives the adjoint and controlled versions, with their relative phases
retained. This proves the claimed norm error and resource bounds.
With W available, number measurement and joint evolution reduce to single-qubit operations
conjugated byW.
Corollary 59(Number measurement and joint evolution).For everyr≥ 1, polynomial-size finite
circuits implement, to operational error at most2−r:
