Conclusion




This study investigated whether variation in interpulse intervals contributes to automated bat-species classification and whether transformer-based models are more sensitive to this information than CNN-based models. The results provide limited evidence that natural IPI variation was a dominant source of species-discriminative information under the conditions examined. BatDetect2 and BAT showed only small differences between natural- and normalized-IPI recordings, while reducing IPI variation did not affect PaSST more strongly than EfficientNet. Instead, within-condition performance remained similar for PaSST and improved for EfficientNet following IPI normalization.






The cross-condition evaluation nevertheless showed that performance obtained under normalized temporal conditions did not transfer fully to natural recordings. When models trained on normalized-IPI recordings were evaluated on matched recordings with natural IPI variation, accuracy decreased for both architectures. This finding indicates sensitivity to differences between the training and evaluation conditions, although it does not establish whether the decrease resulted specifically from IPI variation or from other changes introduced by the normalization procedure.






Overall, the findings suggest that normalizing call spacing can simplify within-condition classification without necessarily improving performance on unmodified recordings. They also demonstrate the importance of evaluating acoustic classifiers on data that retain the natural variability expected during deployment. Larger and more diverse datasets, together with more targeted manipulations of temporal and spectral information, will be needed to determine how sequence context contributes to bat-species classification and whether transformer-based models use it differently from CNN-based models.",cues
