2610.01583v2,Continual Reinforcement Learning with Neuroevolution,"Why does the GA learn new tasks better than ES but forgets more? We hypothesize that both follow from how the GA selects solutions: where ES averages its perturbed copies into a single centroid, the GA keeps its best individuals and mutates them.","6 
Discussion




We compared two neuroevolution methods, ES and a GA, to PPO and its continual variants across environments and kinds of task change, with matched compute and no signal of a switch. ES achieves the best stability-plasticity trade-off.
The GA is the most plastic method in almost every environment, and rewarding novelty increases its plasticity further.
PPO loses plasticity in most environments, and its continual variants restore it at the cost of stability.
Neuroevolution does not show the symptoms of plasticity loss reported in continual RL: its networks do not accumulate dormant units, and the GA’s weights drift further than PPO’s without costing it plasticity, which suggests that these symptoms stem from gradient-based optimisation rather than from the non-stationarity.






We propose a mechanism explaining these results: exploring in parameter space biases learning towards wide neighborhoods, which are more likely to overlap with the neighborhood of the next task: across methods, the size of this shared neighborhood predicts the stability-plasticity trade-off. This builds on evidence that wider neighborhoods reduce forgetting
(
Mirzadeh et al., 2020
)
 and that ES prefers robust solutions 
(
Lehman et al., 2018
)
. To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to propose parameter-space exploration as a mechanism for balancing stability and plasticity in continual RL.






Despite these benefits, NE is also characterized by limitations that may lead to it underperforming RL in some settings. First, it is less sample-efficient, and complex tasks often require large populations 
(
Such et al., 2018
)
.
Second, where good solutions are finely tuned, as in HalfCheetah in our experiments, NE does not match RL’s stationary performance: gradient steps can climb narrow peaks of the return, which ES smooths over 
(
Lehman et al., 2018
)
.
Yet ES has recently been shown impressing scaling, fine-tuning language models with billions of parameters 
(
Qiu et al., 2026b
; 
Sarkar et al., 2026
)
.
Our results are also encouraging: both ES and the GA learn the Kinetix levels with a convolutional policy of more than a million parameters better than RL at the same budget.






Our results open two directions for future work.
First, the two NE mechanisms we found beneficial for continual learning, parameter-space exploration for stability and population diversity for plasticity, may also benefit RL.
For example, injecting noise into the policy’s weights 
(
Plappert et al., 2018
)
 could let PPO follow the gradient of a smoothed return, and population-based methods that reward diversity 
(
Parker-Holder et al., 2020
)
 could keep it plastic.
Second, NE itself can be improved with mechanisms we left out.
For example, ES and GAs can adapt the range of their mutations online 
(
Bck, 1991
; 
Hansen, 2016
; 
Wierstra et al., 2008
)
; raising the mutation rate when performance drops has long been used to track changing environments 
(
Grefenstette, 1992
; 
Pedersen et al., 2025
)
.
Overall, our work introduces a long-standing optimization paradigm to continual RL and proposes novel mechanisms for balancing stability and plasticity.








