GPT-OSS-120B candidate generation and rubric judging ran on an NVIDIA DGX Spark with a GB10 Grace Blackwell Superchip and 128 GB of unified memory. All other experimental work ran on an Intel Core i9-14900KF workstation with an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4080 SUPER.








Limitations




Without sentence-level gold labels for the project corpora, the predictors’ native scores remain uncalibrated to a shared scale. Score gaps and agreement therefore characterize relative behavior, not comparative accuracy.






Candidate selection is bounded to one evaluator, two separate review sessions, the same 60 source cases with three candidates each, two corpora, two generators, and one pre-specified direction-validity rule. The observed arm difference combines candidate-set and session effects.






Comparator identity carries a separate limit: it is a pinned post-publication implementation of Ko 
et al.
’s predictor, not a reproduced 2019 checkpoint or an author-designated replacement for the 2019 Table 2 system. Released-data checks on Twitter, Yelp, and Movie all produced lower rank correlations and higher MAE than the published results, but the unavailable paper-era artifact prevents attribution of the reproduction gap to a particular implementation difference. The changed objective, unseeded augmentation, and unresolved Python run variation limit the project findings to this target-adapted implementation and its Adapt-1/2/3 repetitions.






As a related construct, GranuScore measures semantic and referential granularity rather than descriptive specificity. Some rows receive its exact no-unit sentinel, which the primary analysis retains and a sensitivity analysis excludes. GranuScore therefore provides a construct-sensitive comparison, not a third specificity predictor.






Some sampled originals and author-crafted edits retain source-format remnants or typographical slips; all were scored as written, so deltas may reflect formatting and token changes as well as descriptive detail.






Strict filtering keeps only 35.6%–70.9% of rows and changes the four corpora by unequal amounts. Wider or reordered gaps after filtering can reflect the changed samples, so this condition is a sensitivity check rather than a cleaner replacement dataset.






Length adjustment omits character count and other differences; Python has the weakest common support despite meeting the weight threshold. Adjusted gaps retain other confounding and do not establish a causal or invariant domain effect.






Visible lexical and token-shape cues form the proxy features; they are not semantic, syntactic, ontological, or model-internal explanations. Their correlations can show what travels with a score, but they cannot tell us why the model produced it or what the sentence means.






The 40+40 pilot uses selected sentences, three non-expert annotators, and one score per judge and sentence.






Coverage stops at one general English corpus and three English technical-documentation corpora, scored by SpeciTeller and one pinned post-publication target-adapted comparator. Other documentation types, languages, and predictor families may show different score behavior.






Evaluation is limited to existing language scores and one candidate-selection decision across two candidate sets. Figure 
2
 shows a possible author-controlled use, but its contextual checks, author choice, and task outcomes remain unevaluated. Retrieval, documentation quality, user, team, and collaboration outcomes remain outside the claim scope.








Ethical Considerations




This study evaluates existing linguistic scoring methods across public technical-documentation corpora and English Wikipedia. Three adult annotators were recruited through personal networks and participated voluntarily. They were informed that their anonymized responses would be used for research, provided consent before participating, and received nominal nonmonetary compensation. The procedure posed minimal risk, and no personal or sensitive information was collected. Under institutional guidelines, the study qualified as exempt from formal IRB review.








