Conclusion




Synthetic document fine-tuning has become a common tool for alignment and safety research, but applied to a post-trained model it damages the model’s preference coherence and causes reality drift, while adding the documents to mid-training requires redoing post-training for each experiment.
Questions of model motivations, evaluation awareness and alignment faking presume coherent beliefs, so this damage confounds the reliability of these evaluations.
Somewhat unexpectedly, beliefs learned during SDF on the base model can be transplanted to the latest checkpoint, which suggests that this knowledge is largely orthogonal to what post-training changes.
Our method recovers much of the damage while keeping capabilities, across false facts, model organisms and alignment interventions, with a single adapter reusable across post-trained checkpoints.






We note some key limitations to our approach which invite explorations in future work. 
Limited post-training
: we demonstrate the similarity of grafting to true mid-training with 200k examples of instruction data, only a fraction of production post-training. Future work ought to reproduce larger post-training including RL atop an SDF mid-trained model.

Etiological validity
: ideally, the installed misalignment arises from the same training pressures, dynamics and data as in production models, so that interventions tested on the organism transfer; grafting moves closer to a real mid-training install but does not guarantee these desiderata.

Expression-damage trade-off
: we note that stronger expression is often correlated with greater damage; future work should compare methods at matched expression.
Grafting may also succeed precisely because the SDF objective does not interact much with general post-training, which hints at its limits for path-dependent training that requires the behavior to already be present, such as suppression training against reward hacking.
Future work ought to consider whether grafting generalizes to combining more than one SDF adapter, and to other learning objectives or data formats; our results on emergent misalignment (Section 
5.2
) suggest that instruction-format data are still graftable.
As alignment increasingly relies on shaping how models generalize from post-training, cheap and faithful ways to test pre-training interventions are critical; we hope grafting is one of them.






AI use statement




In this work, we used generative AI tools to implement research software including training and evaluation harnesses specified and drafted by the authors, to manage GPU allocation for jobs executing software, and to provide granular and summary visualizations for results of training and evaluation jobs. We used LLMs to generate feedback on intermediate progress, primarily to spot incorrect implementations vis-a-vis the user specification, as well as to screen evaluations for harness issues. We used LLMs as well to generate tables and figures, with extensive iteration.
We used an LLM to search for related works 
after
 posing the original research idea, namely with respect to model merging for capabilities.






Where specified in the paper, we also use generative AI to synthesize scenarios for behavioral assessment, as well as to judge the responses of models tested according to human-specified rubrics.
We also used generative AI to summarize results for our review.
We reviewed AI-assisted work by reading the code that produces the reported results, sanity-checking results directly, and reading samples of the transcripts from LLM-judged evaluations. We also validated LLM-generated points of feedback on implementation gaps.
We did not use generative AI for conceptual frameworks, hypotheses, experimental design, translation, or qualitative analysis.
All main body prose is our own. The Appendix writing includes prose rephrased by LLMs namely for text captions on tables and figures. LLMs also assisted with updating reported numbers. This work does not feature mathematical claims and thus does not feature assistance on formulation, definitions, or proof writing.
We take responsibility for the final content of this work, including text, claims or artifacts produced with the aid of generative AI.








Ethics statement




We do not anticipate that this research would pose negative consequences to health, personal security or privacy. We believe our findings would aid researchers in understanding and improving the safety of current language models.








Reproducibility statement




The repository at 
github.com/peternutter/grafting-beliefs
 contains the scripts used to train and evaluate models, as well as measurements of results. Our description of methods used follows from those which are implemented in this repository. Appendix section §
A
 reiterates the configurations we used to produce our results and result-specific sections include item-level results from each evaluation and training procedure for transparency.








