2610.00764v1,Teaching a plasma physics and engineering class at a liberal arts college using Course-Based Undergraduate Research Experiences (CUREs),"Using the measured plasma parameters, students estimated the nanoparticle charge and developed the hypothesis that a reduction in electron density leads to a smaller negative dust charge and consequently a weaker electric force available to levitate the particles as a function of increasing magnetic field strength between 50 and 330 Gauss.","7 
Conclusion




In this work, we developed a special-topics course in Plasma Physics and Engineering using a Course-Based Undergraduate Research Experiences at a small liberal arts college. The course was intentionally divided between traditional classroom instruction and an authentic research experience. During the first half of the semester, students developed the fundamental plasma physics knowledge needed to understand dusty plasma and particle levitation. During the second half, they learned plasma diagnostic techniques and applied them in the laboratory to investigate why nanoparticles levitate for a shorter time in the presence of a magnetic field.






An important outcome of the CUREs was that the students were not simply reproducing a known laboratory experiment. Instead, they collected and analyzed experimental data and used their results together with the literature to develop a hypothesis for an unanswered research question. Langmuir probe measurements showed a general decrease in electron density as the magnetic field increased, while OES measurements showed a reduction in the nanoparticle growth-cycle time. Using the measured plasma parameters, students estimated that the dust charge also decreased with increasing magnetic field strength. From these observations, they hypothesized that the decrease in electron density reduces the negative charge on the nanoparticles and consequently reduces the electric force available to levitate them. This provides a possible explanation for the shorter particle levitation time observed in the magnetized plasma, although additional measurements of the plasma and particle properties during nanoparticle growth will be needed to test this hypothesis directly.






The CUREs also provided students with experience beyond the traditional classroom. They assembled and operated plasma experiments, learned Langmuir probes and optical emission spectroscopy diagnostics, analyzed experimental data, read scientific literature, developed hypotheses, and communicated their results. Several students subsequently presented this work at IEEE ICOPS 2026. Student evaluations showed a positive response to combining lecture-based instruction with laboratory research, and four of the five students who completed both E-CLASS surveys reported an increased interest in physics during the semester. Because the course included only six students, these educational results should not be generalized beyond this particular class, but they provide encouraging evidence for this approach.






Overall, this course shows that plasma physics can provide a useful platform for a CUREs because it naturally combines fundamental physics, engineering, experimental diagnostics, computation, and an active research question. A similar approach could be adapted at other undergraduate institutions to broaden students’ exposure to plasma science while providing an authentic research experience within the structure of a course.








Acknowledgement




