Discussion




Motivated by the operational challenges faced by modern electricity grids, we study how demand response pricing can be deployed effectively in realistic, large-scale systems. To assess this question, we extend the existing demand response pricing mechanisms by incorporating rich contextual information into the computation of dynamic prices. Building on the mean-field characterization of Stackelberg equilibrium, we develop a context-aware learning framework that offers a principled way for demand response pricing in large-scale electricity systems. It improves over flat rate and context-agnostic prices particularly in environments with high renewable penetration or significant day-to-day weather fluctuations, highlighting its practical value.






On the other hand, from a methodological perspective, our approach illustrates how the dual characterization of Stackelberg equilibrium enables scalable learning. Rather than repeatedly solving a bi-level optimization problem for each observed state, we learn a constrained mapping from contextual signals to feasible prices. Once trained, this mapping can generate near-optimal prices with minimal computational overhead. This distinction is important for real-time or large-scale implementations, where repeated equilibrium computation may be impractical. Besides these benefits, such formulation also introduces algorithmic challenges of constrained output learning. Our proposed cluster-based soft classifier algorithm provides one way to balance computational efficiency and adaptability to constraints.






Several limitations remain. First, our framework relies on the accuracy and representativeness of contextual features; mis-specified or poorly predicted exogenous factors may degrade performance. In addition, while mean-field theory provides asymptotic guarantees, finite-population effects may cause behavior synchronization and introduce deviations from optimality.






Future work could focus on developing robust models that explicitly account for uncertainty in contextual features and produce interval or probabilistic pricing rules. For instance, integrating probabilistic forecasts or Bayesian neural networks for exogenous factors would quantify forecast uncertainty in the inputs. This allows the framework to be more robust against mis-specification and make risk-aware decisions. Another interesting direction is to extend the framework to incorporate strategic consumer behavior beyond passive price response. For example, consumers could be modeled as forward-looking agents who optimize intertemporal utility under comfort constraints, while anticipating pricing policies. Such extensions would enable the framework to capture endogenous demand shifting due to strategic behavior, leading to more sophisticated equilibrium.






