Jing and Tian, 2019
; 
Balestriero et al., 2023
)
.","5 
Discussion




We investigated the effect of width on the performance of networks trained with greedy layer-wise training, asking for what architectures a network trained with restricted credit assignment can produce representations comparable in performance to networks trained end-to-end. Across four and eight layer convolutional neural networks trained with the Barlow Twins loss on the CIFAR-10 dataset, increasing network width disproportionately improved the categorization accuracy (assessed with a 
k
k
NN probe) of greedy layer-wise trained networks compared to end-to-end trained networks. Greedy training matched or even surpassed end-to-end training in terms of model performance for the widest models. Additional experiments showed a similar performance convergence for networks trained with SimCLR contrastive loss, and with a different dataset (CIFAR-100 instead of CIFAR-10), thus showing that our findings are not specific to Barlow Twins loss or to the CIFAR-10 dataset.






Our representational geometry analysis provides a possible explanation for these differences. Greedy layer-wise trained networks reached higher signal–signal factorization and signal–noise factorization in our widest networks, indicating that these representations distributed class related variation more evenly and had stronger class signal relative to within-class variation. This is consistent with the theoretical work of 
Wakhloo et al. (2026)
, who show that lower classification error is possible as SSF and SNF increase, as well as related work showing that categorization error is related to the overlap between signal and noise directions in representation space 
(
Sorscher et al., 2022
)
.






Why greedy layer-wise trained networks produce representations with better SSF and SNF than ones with end-to-end training is not fully known. However, one possibility is that freezing earlier layers may preserve class-related structure that end-to-end optimization might alter over lengthy training on SSL loss functions (which do not explicitly reinforce categorical representations). This hypothesis is consistent with our observation of SSF declining over the latest epochs during end-to-end training. Future research should further explore how local learning, network width, and layer freezing shape representational geometry.






Our results suggest that increasing network width can compensate somewhat for a lack of global credit assignment. This finding is particularly relevant for the exploration of biologically plausible alternatives to backpropagation. Backpropagation is often cited as biologically implausible due to its globally coordinated credit assignment. Exploring relatively shallow, wide networks with local learning is a biologically motivated alternative: the brain has relatively few processing stages (shallow architecture) 
(
Suzuki et al., 2023
; 
Harris et al., 2019
; 
D’Souza et al., 2022
)
 with massive neural populations (wide architecture) 
(
Babadi and Sompolinsky, 2014
; 
Litwin-Kumar et al., 2017
)
. Thus, our results motivate the further exploration of wide, shallow networks optimized with local learning rules. More broadly, our results support the investigation of width as a potential factor to influence the performance of biologically plausible learning rules.






Our work has several limitations. First, our tested architectures are limited to relatively shallow convolutional networks. Greater depth or architectural differences, such as residual connections, could result in a different relationship between width and performance in greedy layer-wise training. At the same time, our main finding generalized well between architectures of quite different depth (4 vs 8 layers): in both cases, increasing width shrinks the gap between networks trained end-to-end with backprop vs greedy layer-wise methods. We thus anticipate that this same effect would apply more broadly. We hope that future work explores these ideas in a wider range of contexts, broadening our perspective on when and why localized credit assignment may produce representations of comparable quality to end-to-end backpropagation of error.






Next, our experiments were limited to two loss functions and two datasets. We primarily studied networks trained with the Barlow Twins loss function on the CIFAR-10 dataset. At the same time, our primary results also held for a different loss function and dataset, suggesting that our findings are likely generalizable across additional datasets and choices of loss function. Finally, our study is limited to self-supervised learning, and we did not investigate whether a similar width-dependent result is present in other contexts such as supervised learning. Nevertheless, the central role of self-supervised learning in representation pretraining makes it an important setting in which to study alternative training paradigms.






Overall, we identified an architectural condition where greedy layer-wise training can produce representations of comparable quality to those learned through end-to-end backpropagation. We show that increased width benefits greedy layer-wise training, narrowing the performance gap with end-to-end backpropagation. We point to differences in representational geometry as a potential reason for improved greedy layer-wise representations. Together, these results motivate width as a primary variable determining the efficacy of local learning paradigms.






