We investigated whether frozen, zero-shot LLMs can

provide effective hierarchical cyber defense across network scales without task-specific retraining
 and how performance changes as LLM control extends from strategic planning to tactical execution.
Across Cyberwheel networks, planner-only LLM integration provides limited gains at larger scales, whereas capable LLMs controlling both levels achieve strong cross-scale performance. Smaller and some cybersecurity-specialized models degrade as networks grow, indicating that domain specialization does not guarantee scale generalization. Adding pretrained reasoning only at the top of a hierarchical defender may be insufficient; effective tactical execution is important for translating strategic decisions into defensive performance.






