We compared hierarchical red team architectures, RL+RL and LLM+LLM, across CybORG CAGE-4 and Cyberwheel under one disruption metric and found that neither dominates. The stronger architecture inverts with the environment. An all-RL agent wins the compact, densely rewarded CAGE-4 and the 100-host Cyberwheel network, and a cyber-pretrained all-LLM agent wins the large, escalation-gated 1010-host Cyberwheel network. Decomposing the attack progression revealed that every architecture reaches host compromise, and privilege escalation is the stage at which each fails, i.e., RL does not learn to escalate on the 1010-host Cyberwheel network, and the language models gain root but rarely convert it to impact on CAGE-4. The practical reading is a conditions-based guideline, as follows: (1) prefer an RL learned policy when the reward is dense and escalation is reachable from the reward signal, and (2) prefer a capable, cyber-pretrained language model when the network is large and escalation depends on prior tactical knowledge the reward never supplies.






