Discussion




In this paper, we introduced Heteroskedastic CP (HCP) Decomposition, an extension of CP decomposition to an entrywise independent Gaussian model with unknown, low-rank precision tensor. We jointly estimate the mean and precision by alternating between the weighted CP and Gamma-rate CP subproblems, deriving block-coordinate updates for each, and address the degeneracy of the objective with a residual modification that corresponds to a penalized likelihood approach.
Across synthetic experiments, we see that HCP is substantially more stable than CP or GCP decomposition under increasing heteroskedasticity, and we demonstrate the advantages of our “two-parameter” decomposition in handling model misspecification and providing interpretability benefits.






Addressing the assumptions of our model provides many clear avenues for future work. The entrywise independence could be replaced with a covariance from the tensor-multivariate-normal distribution 
Manceur and Dutilleul (2013)
, the Gaussian generating distribution could be replaced with one allowing heavy tails, such as Student’s t distribution, taking inspiration from 
Auddy and Yuan (2022)
, or other low-rank models (e.g. Tucker, Tensor Train) could be considered. Other two parameter distributions may also be of interest. For example, the non-negative tensor factorizations developed by 
Chi and Kolda (2012)
 would likely not be adaptive to over-dispersed regimes, mirroring CP’s degeneracy under heteroskedasticity.








