This paper develops a general approach to computing and estimating sharp bounds on partially identified averages. The framework accommodates unobserved heterogeneity represented by general structural response functions, together with distributional restrictions indexed by a potentially continuous range of observed conditions. Under our regularity conditions, we show that the identification problem is equivalent to a constrained optimal transport problem on the space of admissible response paths. This representation preserves the identifying information in the observable distributions while making the role of structural assumptions explicit.






