Conclusion




We presented 
DART
, a runtime defense that detects emerging multi-turn safety risks from representation transitions, attributes them to the contributing context, and intervenes with targeted reminders. Across six models and two agentic benchmarks, 
DART
 substantially reduces attack success while preserving benign utility, including attacks composed entirely of individually permissible steps. Denoising is critical for separating sparse harmful shifts from accumulated benign drift, enabling reliable trajectory-level monitoring at low runtime cost. By reusing the agent’s hidden states, 
DART
 provides a lightweight safety layer that can operate independently or complement heavier defenses such as speculative simulation.








