Conclusion and Future Work




We reveal a post-update phenomenon in which training on aligned samples can transfer learned behaviors to contexts where those behaviors are misaligned, which we call 
context confusion
. We demonstrate this phenomenon across three important domains: Gender Equality, Privacy, and Physical Safety. We further evaluate context confusion extensively and provide a mechanistic explanation for why it occurs. For future work, identifying additional domains where context confusion may occur, developing robust and generalizable solutions to prevent it, and understanding how fine-tuning data may affect model alignment before training are important research directions.








