Discussion




This work demonstrates the possibility of improving agentic oversight by continuously training an AI monitor.
We argue that this ongoing ”co-training” allows the monitor to adapt to worker behavior, in the same settings where a fixed monitor would be easily exploited.
Moreover, we provide some theoretical grounding for this setting by establishing a tight connection to adversarial online learning and thereby characterize the conditions needed for effective monitoring. Our experiments demonstrate that co-training monitors, even with a vanishing query schedule, can suppress worker attacks.
We also discuss implications of monitoring for ensuring overall aligned worker behavior – the feasibility of alignment via monitoring depends on the relative cost and payoff of aligned and misaligned actions for the worker.






Limitations and open directions.




Our experiments provide evidence in restricted task settings. Limited computational resources prevented evaluation across a broader range of tasks, larger models, and longer training runs. Whether a single monitor can maintain reliable oversight across diverse domains and sustained adversarial adaptation remains open. Our comparisons test co-training against fixed-monitor feedback, but do not establish whether either approach improves on training without monitor feedback.






Our supervised guarantees assume reliable ground-truth labels and a fixed judgment function within an online-learnable class. They do not establish robustness to noisy or misspecified supervision, or that practical neural-network training achieves the theoretical guarantees. Moreover, vanishing average error does not preclude substantial harm before the monitor learns, or rare failures with severe consequences. Also, there are other schemes for choosing when to query the ground truth that we did not explore in the main text: see Appendix 
E
 for some additional experiments.






Our self-supervised analysis establishes sharpening toward the monitor’s initial majority judgments, rather than a general guarantee of improved correctness. Sharpening can improve single-verdict accuracy under the stated conditions, but can also reinforce systematic errors. The guarantee further requires action coverage and controlled fitting error, limiting the adversarial strategies it accommodates. Compute-amplified co-training remains experimentally unvalidated in this work.






Finally, our analysis primarily establishes monitor guarantees and derives their consequences for worker incentives. The honest-completion result assumes favorable payoffs and a near-optimal sequential worker response; it does not show that a particular training algorithm learns that response. A fuller game-theoretic treatment of joint worker-monitor learning could clarify when co-training converges to useful behavior, when strategic interactions undermine it, and how rewards and auditing policies should be designed.








